  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1203 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nAfter a few days of northeasterly to east-northeasterly winds, the \nflow is expected to shift back to the southeast as upper troughing \npropagates over the Rockies and Central US. This brings a return of \ncloudiness to South Central Texas starting Wednesday, and low \nchances (mostly below 20 percent) for isolated streamer showers \nstarting Thursday with the best chances over the Rio Grande Plains \nand Winter Garden region where the southeasterly flow will be most \nevident. Dew points will rise with this change in flow. We have \novernight lows increasing quite noticeably into the upper 50s to low \n60s Wednesday night, becoming more mild than average. Highs will \nsettle into the 60s and 70s by Wednesday, and these warmer \nconditions should continue through at least Saturday. \n\nThe general consensus this weekend among the deterministic and \nensemble models is that the local area will be in the space between \nthe polar jet stream to the north and the subtropical jet stream to \nthe south. This configuration reduces the likelihood of a strong \ndisturbance arriving over the weekend. However, persistent troughing \nover the northern half of the western US suggests a weak cold front \nmay drop south near the region. Weak disturbances riding along \neither the subtropical or polar jet should gradually increase \ncoastal surface troughing, with added moist air pushing over the \nfrontal surface and providing  additional chances for rains this \nupcoming weekend. The GEFS, GEPS, ENS, and AIFS-ENS for now all \nsuggest Sunday may provide the best rain chances in the next seven \ndays, though given the synoptic factors in play this timing may vary \nconsiderably.",
